---

[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'When I’m out on a mountain hike, as I was&nbsp; here, I know exactly which camera I’d choose&nbsp;&nbsp; between the Insta360 Luna Ultra and the&nbsp; X6, and which of them is better suited&nbsp;&nbsp; to this purpose. But more on that later. The Insta360 X6 and the Luna Ultra are two&nbsp;&nbsp; very different cameras, and both have their own&nbsp; unique strengths. But since I know many of you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are interested in a direct comparison, I’d like&nbsp; to show you a few longer comparison clips today&nbsp;&nbsp; and highlight the strengths of both cameras. In&nbsp; fact, both cameras also have things in common:&nbsp;&nbsp; they’re both well-suited for vlogging and, of&nbsp; course, for family or vacation shots. And we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see that they’re also similarly priced. But let’s first take a look at the key&nbsp;&nbsp; differences—after that, I’ll show you&nbsp; several comparison shots so you can&nbsp;&nbsp; better decide for yourself which camera&nbsp; is the right choice for you. At the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I’ll tell you which camera I’d recommend to whom. The most important difference between the Luna&nbsp;&nbsp; Ultra and the x6 is, of course, that the Luna&nbsp; Ultra is a conventional camera that captures&nbsp;&nbsp; flat footage, while the x6 is a 360 camera.&nbsp; The x6 has a camera on each side with an&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extremely wide-angle lens. The footage from&nbsp; both cameras is then stitched together into&nbsp;&nbsp; a 360 image. This has several advantages: you&nbsp; can look around freely during playback, or you&nbsp;&nbsp; can choose the perfect framing in post and then&nbsp; export a regular flat clip. And as a 360 camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the x6 has another interesting advantage: the&nbsp; selfie stick automatically disappears from&nbsp;&nbsp; the frame. Combined with the reframing feature,&nbsp; this allows for interesting and creative shots.&nbsp; Not only that—you take just one shot and&nbsp; get an endless number of angles to work&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with. The downside is that, under normal&nbsp; circumstances, you still have to edit and&nbsp;&nbsp; reframe 360° shots. Insta360 has worked hard&nbsp; to minimize this drawback, and the X6 features&nbsp;&nbsp; several functions—such as Auto Edit and the AI&nbsp; Director—designed to handle the reframing for&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you. However, the fact remains that reframing is&nbsp; essentially a significant crop of the footage. As&nbsp;&nbsp; a result, the reframed footage isn’t in 4K; the&nbsp; exported clip typically has a resolution of 2.7K.&nbsp;&nbsp; If needed, you can also use the X6 to capture&nbsp; flat 4K footage with just one lens in “Single&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Lens Mode,” which then doesn’t require reframing.&nbsp; And it’s precisely in this area that the X6 has&nbsp;&nbsp; made great progress. It’s capable of capturing&nbsp; flat 4K footage at up to 120 frames per second.&nbsp; The Luna Ultra also has two cameras: one&nbsp; with a wide-angle lens and a large 1” sensor,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and one with a zoom lens that has a focal length&nbsp; of 60mm. But what really sets the Luna Ultra&nbsp;&nbsp; apart is its gimbal. Both cameras are mounted on a&nbsp; physical gimbal, which offers several advantages.&nbsp;&nbsp; The recordings can be physically stabilized, so&nbsp; the camera doesn’t have to rely on electronic&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stabilization. This enables particularly stable&nbsp; footage in low light or when using the optimal&nbsp;&nbsp; slow shutter speed—something that’s difficult&nbsp; to achieve with electronic stabilization.&nbsp;&nbsp; There’s another important advantage, which I’ll&nbsp; demonstrate later. Of course, the gimbal also has&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a drawback. The Luna Ultra isn’t an action camera&nbsp; and, of course, isn’t waterproof. In contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp; the x6 is waterproof up to 20 meters without an&nbsp; additional case, has a rugged body that can be&nbsp;&nbsp; easily mounted anywhere—even on a helmet or chest&nbsp; mount—and the lenses can be replaced if needed.&nbsp;'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That means, just in terms of the body and&nbsp; features alone, the x6 has many advantages. The&nbsp;&nbsp; Luna Ultra’s greatest strength lies in its image&nbsp; quality. And as you can see here, it’s difficult&nbsp;&nbsp; to compare the footage from the two cameras. Both&nbsp; shots look very good on their own. However, there&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are two major differences between the shots from&nbsp; the two cameras. The first is quite obvious:&nbsp;&nbsp; this shot from the Luna Ultra was taken with the&nbsp; wide-angle main lens. With a 20 mm focal length,&nbsp;&nbsp; this lens has a relatively wide field of view.&nbsp; But as you can see here, the x6’s field of view&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is significantly wider. This naturally leads to&nbsp; distortion, but the extremely wide field of view&nbsp;&nbsp; allows you to capture a lot of the surrounding&nbsp; area. It’s particularly well-suited for action&nbsp;&nbsp; shots or shots where you want to include yourself&nbsp; in the landscape or during sports activities.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The extremely wide field of view, on the other&nbsp; hand, isn’t well-suited for close-ups of people,&nbsp;&nbsp; since the distortion becomes particularly&nbsp; noticeable in these cases. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can crop a shot digitally to enlarge it.&nbsp; However, this results in reduced image quality&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and is only recommended up to a certain point. For the Luna Ultra, there’s also an additional&nbsp;&nbsp; ultra-wide lens that you can attach magnetically&nbsp; to the main lens. The ultra-wide lens provides&nbsp;&nbsp; an even wider field of view, allowing you&nbsp; to capture a bit more of the landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even though the difference compared to the main&nbsp; lens isn’t that significant. Here you can see a&nbsp;&nbsp; comparison between a shot taken with the x6 and&nbsp; one taken with the Luna Ultra using the ultra-wide&nbsp;&nbsp; lens. And you can see just how extremely wide&nbsp; the x6’s field of view is. A major strength&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the Luna Ultra in this comparison is that it&nbsp; also features a second camera and a second 60mm&nbsp;&nbsp; lens. With a 60mm focal length, you can not only&nbsp; get significantly closer to subjects, it is also&nbsp;&nbsp; well-suited for portrait shots—that is, close-ups&nbsp; of faces. You can’t take shots like these with the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'X6. But that’s not all. The Luna Ultra offers&nbsp; a lossless 2x crop on both the main lens and&nbsp;&nbsp; the zoom lens. This enables a 120mm focal length&nbsp; without any significant loss of image quality.&nbsp;&nbsp; The Luna Ultra’s maximum zoom is 12x. This means&nbsp; that, purely in terms of focal lengths, the Luna&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ultra offers significantly more possibilities&nbsp; and flexibility than the X6. On top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp; the Luna Ultra can also shoot in 8K—8K that you&nbsp; don’t have to reframe. This means you get footage&nbsp;&nbsp; that you can crop quite heavily if needed. So the&nbsp; question is: do you prefer the very wide look, or&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do you need more flexibility with focal lengths? Aside from the focal length, there’s another&nbsp;&nbsp; important difference between the shots from&nbsp; the two cameras. The x6 has a fixed focus set&nbsp;&nbsp; to infinity. That means everything is in focus&nbsp; and sharp—the person in the foreground as well&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as the landscape in the background. This works&nbsp; very well for action shots or shots where you&nbsp;&nbsp; want to show both the person and the landscape in&nbsp; the background at the same time. Everything is in&nbsp;&nbsp; focus, and even with fast movements, there are no&nbsp; issues with the autofocus. The Luna Ultra, on the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other hand, uses a very good autofocus system.&nbsp; It focuses on the subject in the foreground. And&nbsp;&nbsp; because the Luna Ultra’s main lens has a large 1”&nbsp; sensor and a very wide F1.8 aperture, it creates&nbsp;&nbsp; a relatively strong background blur. And that&nbsp; looks very soft, high-quality, and cinematic.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This background blur effect is constantly used in&nbsp; professional movies and is also typical of larger,&nbsp;&nbsp; high-end cameras. That’s why we perceive it as&nbsp; so cinematic. And it’s precisely this look—which&nbsp;&nbsp; can also be achieved well with the 3x telephoto&nbsp; lens—that’s a particular strength of the Luna&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ultra—but you have to like it. And I know that&nbsp; many people prefer everything to be sharp and&nbsp;&nbsp; in focus, like on the X6. Personally, I like&nbsp; the background blur, and it looks especially&nbsp;&nbsp; good when shooting close-ups. But for sports or&nbsp; travel, it’s also very useful when everything&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is in focus—after all, you want to see&nbsp; as much as possible in your recordings.&nbsp; The Luna Ultra’s autofocus and zoom lens, combined&nbsp; with a relatively short minimum focusing distance,&nbsp;&nbsp; also allow for macro-like close-ups of&nbsp; subjects or people. These look great, too.&nbsp;'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Personally, I like both—the wide-angle look&nbsp; of the X6 where everything is in focus,&nbsp;&nbsp; but also the cinematic, soft look with background&nbsp; blur for high-quality shots. I think you can&nbsp;&nbsp; already see where this is going. Both cameras&nbsp; have very different strengths and complement&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each other rather than serving as alternatives to&nbsp; one another. While the X6 is an outstanding action&nbsp;&nbsp; camera, the Luna Ultra is designed from the ground&nbsp; up to achieve optimal image quality. By the way,&nbsp;&nbsp; both cameras support I-LOG in 10-bit and Dolby&nbsp; Vision HDR. What do you prefer—a very wide field&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of view with everything in focus, like on the X6,&nbsp; or multiple focal lengths and background blur?&nbsp; 2. For a 360 camera, the X6 takes very good shots&nbsp; in low light, but it can’t quite keep up with the&nbsp;&nbsp; Luna Ultra, especially when it gets very dark.&nbsp; Under these conditions, the large sensor and the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gimbal are simply a decisive advantage, since the&nbsp; Luna Ultra can reduce the shutter speed without&nbsp;&nbsp; causing stabilization issues. But especially&nbsp; when there’s enough artificial light or it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp; quite that dark yet, the X6 also takes very good&nbsp; shots, and the difference isn’t that significant.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The Luna Ultra really starts to stand out only&nbsp; when it gets very, very dark. The gimbal is a&nbsp;&nbsp; major advantage in low light—but how does&nbsp; it perform in good lighting conditions?&nbsp; 3. When the lighting conditions are good,&nbsp; even the best gimbal can’t compete with&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the electronic stabilization of a 360&nbsp; camera. The extremely wide field of view&nbsp;&nbsp; is a decisive advantage when it comes to&nbsp; image stabilization. That’s another reason&nbsp;&nbsp; why the X6 is such an outstanding action camera. On top of that, in the so-called Instaframe mode,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the x6 even has a gimbal mode that simulates how&nbsp; a gimbal works. This means that in this mode,&nbsp;&nbsp; the camera captures a flat—not 360-degree—shot&nbsp; while simulating camera movements of a gimbal.&nbsp; Similar to the Luna Ultra, there’s a Follow&nbsp; mode, an FPV mode, and so on. And we’ll come&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back to this Instaframe mode in a moment. 4. The Luna Ultra’s gimbal offers a very&nbsp;&nbsp; special feature. You can set the camera&nbsp; up anywhere and then have it track you or&nbsp;&nbsp; another subject completely automatically. That’s&nbsp; cool and especially useful for solo creators.&nbsp;'

[08:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And since we’re on the topic of solo creators and&nbsp; tracking: As you may know, you can easily detach&nbsp;&nbsp; the Luna Ultra’s screen and use it like a remote&nbsp; with a display. Of course, this also works in&nbsp;&nbsp; combination with the zoom lens. That means you can&nbsp; have the camera track you from a distance, start&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and stop recording, or even adjust all the camera&nbsp; settings. For me, this is one of the best features&nbsp;&nbsp; of the Luna Ultra. Here, for example, I mounted&nbsp; the Luna Ultra on a Throwpod and then had it track&nbsp;&nbsp; me. By the way, the Throwpod is one of the best&nbsp; accessories for both the Luna Ultra and the X6.&nbsp;'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There are also various remote controls&nbsp; available for the X6, and of course you&nbsp;&nbsp; can connect both cameras to a smartphone&nbsp; if needed and then control them remotely.&nbsp; And you can also mount the X6 on a tripod,&nbsp; like the Throwpod, to film and track&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yourself. There are two ways to do this. You&nbsp; can shoot a 360° video and then track yourself&nbsp;&nbsp; in post—or you can use the Instaframe mode I&nbsp; just mentioned. Similar to the Luna Ultra, the&nbsp;&nbsp; camera locks onto a subject in Instaframe mode and&nbsp; tracks it. However, the result isn’t a 360° video&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but rather a reframed flat clip right. This means&nbsp; you don’t have to edit the footage anymore. You&nbsp;&nbsp; can simply set up the x6 somewhere, let it track&nbsp; you, and you’ll get a reframed flat clip in 4K&nbsp;&nbsp; right away. Extremely useful. But of course, the&nbsp; result isn’t comparable in every respect. The main&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'difference between the two cameras lies simply in&nbsp; the image quality, the different zoom factors, and&nbsp;&nbsp; the distinct look created by a blurred background. In a direct comparison, there are a number of&nbsp;&nbsp; other differences as well. For example, only the&nbsp; x6 features Voice Control, Gesture Control, or the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'new AI Director, which—at the end of the day while&nbsp; charging—automatically creates a sort of daily&nbsp;&nbsp; vlog from the best shots of the day. And then, of&nbsp; course, there’s the topic of photos. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp; there are similar differences here regarding&nbsp; focal lengths and background blur. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only the Luna Ultra lets you take good portrait&nbsp; photos and close-ups with a beautiful background&nbsp;&nbsp; blur, as shown in this example here. On the other&nbsp; hand, 360 cameras are often used for 360 photos&nbsp;&nbsp; for virtual tours. And the X6 takes excellent 360&nbsp; photos with up to 120 megapixels. Interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Luna Ultra can do this as well. It’s not&nbsp; quite as fast, but it’s possible. The camera&nbsp;&nbsp; takes photos in all directions and then stitches&nbsp; them together into a 360-photo. This means that in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'photo mode, you can achieve a result similar to&nbsp; that of a dedicated 360-degree camera. In other&nbsp;&nbsp; words, the Luna Ultra is also suitable for taking&nbsp; 360 photos, for example, for virtual tours. But&nbsp;&nbsp; when it comes to virtual tours in particular, the&nbsp; X6 has a very special feature: 3D Spatial Capture.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can create a fully virtual walkable model from&nbsp; a 360-degree shot. So it’s not just a photo where&nbsp;&nbsp; you can look around, but a model where you can&nbsp; move around freely. This, of course, takes virtual&nbsp;&nbsp; tours to a whole new level. Unfortunately,&nbsp; the feature isn’t currently available in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the U.S. yet due to technical reasons. Personally, I really like both cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp; They’re both examples of very well-designed&nbsp; devices. However, neither of them is exactly&nbsp;&nbsp; cheap. The Insta360 Luna Ultra costs $770&nbsp; in the US and €730 in Europe. The X6 costs&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '$699 in the US and €699 in Europe. So they’re&nbsp; both priced similarly. You’ll find links to&nbsp;&nbsp; both cameras in the video description. And&nbsp; only if you use these links will you receive&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an exclusive free gift with either camera. For the X6, this is a Dual Lens Kit that lets&nbsp;&nbsp; you replace two damaged lenses&nbsp; yourself. For the Luna Ultra,&nbsp;&nbsp; it’s currently the extremely useful Throwpod.&nbsp; But these offers are always time-limited,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so you should hurry if you’re interested. So which of the two cameras should you&nbsp;&nbsp; buy—which is the better choice for you? The answer&nbsp; is relatively clear to me: if you’re into sports,&nbsp;&nbsp; travel a lot, and are looking for a camera that’s&nbsp; rugged and waterproof—and that you can also use&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well for vlogging—then go with the X6—especially&nbsp; if you like the ultra-wide-angle look. The x6 is&nbsp;&nbsp; also great for particularly creative shots. For&nbsp; me, it’s one of the best cameras you can buy right&nbsp;&nbsp; now. If the rugged and waterproof body isn’t&nbsp; that important to you and you’re looking for a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'higher-end, more cinematic and professional&nbsp; look—or want more flexibility with focal&nbsp;&nbsp; lengths—then go for the Luna Ultra. Of course,&nbsp; the Luna Ultra is also excellent for vlogging and&nbsp;&nbsp; if you want to film yourself frequently. Unlike&nbsp; the X6, the Luna Ultra is also very well-suited,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for example, as the main camera for a&nbsp; YouTube channel to shoot talking head shots.&nbsp; But as I said, I think both cameras complement&nbsp; each other well, and it’s fantastic to have&nbsp;&nbsp; both. If you’re still undecided, you’ll&nbsp; find a few files available for download&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the download folder for my Premium Content&nbsp; Channel members, so you can compare the original&nbsp;&nbsp; recordings for yourself on your own screen. Give me a like as feedback if you found this&nbsp;&nbsp; video interesting. You can already find a detailed&nbsp; comparison between the X6, the X5, and the Osmo360&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on my channel. Check it out if you’re interested. There will be more videos coming up on the&nbsp;&nbsp; Luna Ultra and the X6, so stay&nbsp; tuned and see you next time!'
